%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Clientelism is an exchange: a party delivers a private benefit and a voter delivers electoral support \parencite{Kitschelt2000,Nichter2008,Hicken2011}. Yet in most theories of that exchange only one side names the terms. In the distributive-politics tradition, parties decide whom to target and how much to give \parencite{Cox1986,Lindbeck1987,Dixit1996,Stokes2005}. In formal models of vote buying, candidates or interest groups bid for votes and voters respond to the bids \parencite{Groseclose1996,Dekel2008,MorganVardy2011,DalBo2007}. The voter's role is to accept or decline.

A growing body of evidence suggests that this division of roles is too narrow. Citizens request benefits from politicians and brokers \parencite{Nichter2014a}, declare their support to signal that they are worth rewarding \parencite{NichterNunnari2022}, choose among competing brokers \parencite{Auerbach2018}, and, when asked, attach explicit prices to their votes \parencite{Hicken2015,Gherghina2026}. Ethnographic work describes clientelist relationships that are initiated by clients rather than patrons \parencite{Hagene2015,Tosoni2007,Gay1999}. If voters can propose terms, a basic public-choice question follows: \emph{does it matter who sets the price of a vote?} Bargaining theory gives a clear answer in general---the side that makes a take-it-or-leave-it proposal captures the surplus---but whether that answer survives in electoral exchange, where the value of a vote depends on whether it can change the outcome, is an open question. Observational data cannot answer it, because the settings in which voters initiate exchange differ from those in which parties do in many other ways.

This paper isolates the price-setting institution. We study two games that share preferences, budgets, the electorate, information, and payoffs, and differ only in who proposes the price---that is, in the institution, which in experimental economics denotes the rules that govern how participants exchange messages and how those messages become allocations \parencite{Smith1982}. With \emph{price-setting parties} (vote buying), two parties simultaneously post offers and the voter takes one or none. With a \emph{price-setting voter} (vote selling), the voter posts a price to each party, each party accepts or rejects, and the voter then takes one of the accepted prices or none. In both games a completed transaction binds the vote, and a party pays only if the voter takes its offer.

We first characterize the subgame-perfect equilibria of both games. Two results organize the analysis. The first is that, in an environment in which a single vote can tip an election, a vote is worth the full value of office to \emph{either} party when it is pivotal and nothing when it is not. When budgets are smaller than the value of office, each party's willingness to pay is simply its budget when the voter is pivotal and zero otherwise. An implication is that party loss aversion operates through parties' beliefs about pivotality: when those beliefs are correct, the budget binds before loss aversion can raise willingness to pay any further. The second result concerns the institution. With price-setting parties, the two parties engage in asymmetric price competition: the voter's ideologically preferred party wins the vote and pays only what is needed to match the rival's best offer net of the voter's ideological attachment. With a price-setting voter, the voter makes an ultimatum-like demand and extracts the whole budget. The difference---the voter's \emph{proposer advantage}---equals the voter's ideological attachment, capped by the budget. In our parameter draws, the predicted advantage averages \Sexpr{f0(rt$pred.gap.piv)} points for pivotal voters, about \Sexpr{f0(pred.gap.pct)} percent of the average budget. Because each institution was played in different sessions with their own draws, the benchmark for the comparison between the two sets of sessions is \Sexpr{f0(Gr$bench)} points.

We then bring both institutions to the laboratory, where we can do what observational data cannot: keep the voter, the parties, and the stakes the same, and change only who names the price. In \Sexpr{sAll} sessions in \redacted{Santiago, Chile}, \Sexpr{nAll} participants played one of the two games over several rounds, taking turns as the voter and as one of the two parties. Participants earned experimental points that were paid out in cash, so every offer, request, and refusal had real consequences for their earnings. The first prediction of the model holds up well: in both games, participants treat pivotal votes as more valuable than non-pivotal ones. The central prediction does not. Pivotal voters who set their own price earn about as much as pivotal voters who wait for the parties' offers: the difference is \Sexpr{f0(Gr$est)} points, against the \Sexpr{f0(Gr$bench)} points the model predicts. This is not a matter of too little data. The experiment can detect a gain little more than half the size of the predicted one, and the predicted gain is clearly rejected (\autoref{sec:results}).

Why does price-setting power not translate into higher earnings? The answer lies in two behavioral regularities, one on each side of the market, that pull in opposite directions. Parties pay for votes that cannot change the outcome. A non-pivotal vote is worth nothing to them, yet about half of the parties facing such a voter offer something, and about half of the requests from such voters are accepted. Voters, for their part, are reluctant to use the power they are given. A pivotal voter who names the price could ask for a party's entire budget and expect to get it; instead, the typical request is about half of the budget, and few voters ask for all of it. This caution is not misplaced: parties turn down nearly a quarter of the requests that, in equilibrium, they should accept. The pattern will be familiar from the ultimatum game, in which responders reject offers they find too greedy and proposers, anticipating this, moderate their demands \parencite{Guth1982,Roth1991,Camerer2003}. The net result is a balance. Parties' generosity toward non-pivotal voters raises voters' earnings when parties set the price; voters' caution lowers them when voters set the price; and the two effects cancel out.

Why would a party pay for a vote that cannot decide the election? The model suggests an answer. If parties act as if a non-pivotal vote might still be decisive, the budget no longer caps what they are willing to pay, and loss aversion---the tendency to weigh a possible defeat more heavily than an equivalent gain---begins to shape their offers. This account fits a pattern we document below: departures from equilibrium are concentrated on votes that cannot change the outcome, and parties treat those votes as if they had some value, but a limited one.

The paper makes three contributions. First, it offers a tractable model that nests vote buying and vote selling as two price-setting institutions for the same exchange and derives the voter's proposer advantage in closed form. Second, it provides a controlled experimental comparison of the two institutions, which field and survey data cannot provide. Third, it shows that the institutional allocation of price-setting power matters much less in practice than theory predicts, because the behavioral frictions on the two sides of the market move earnings in opposite directions. For students of clientelism, the implication is that voter-initiated exchange need not empower voters. For the public-choice study of political markets, the results caution against inferring the distribution of rents from the formal assignment of bargaining power alone.

The question is a public-choice question in a precise sense. Clientelism is a market in which a public good---the electoral outcome---is traded for private transfers, and the incidence of the rents created by that trade depends on the rules that govern the market as well as on preferences and endowments. Much of the comparative literature treats the rules as given and focuses on the buyer's targeting problem. Our analysis instead holds preferences and endowments fixed and asks how much the allocation of rents depends on a single rule: which side proposes. Because the answer in theory is ``a great deal'' and the answer in our data is ``very little,'' the paper also illustrates a broader point about political markets: formal bargaining power and realized rents can come apart when the participants on each side deviate from equilibrium in predictable ways.

The paper proceeds as follows. \autoref{sec:literature} brings together three literatures that rarely speak to each other: empirical work on clientelism, which increasingly sees voters as active participants in the exchange; formal models of vote buying, which treat parties as the ones who set the price; and laboratory research on bargaining, which shows how people depart from equilibrium when they make or answer take-it-or-leave-it offers. \autoref{sec:model} develops the model. It characterizes the equilibria of both games, derives the voter's proposer advantage, and turns the leading behavioral accounts---misperceived pivotality, loss aversion, noisy responses, and the risk of rejection---into testable signatures. These benchmark predictions were derived after data collection, and we use them as a yardstick against which to read behavior. \autoref{sec:design} describes the experiment and the empirical strategy. \autoref{sec:results} follows the argument step by step: it first shows how behavior responds to pivotality, then documents the two deviations, one on each side of the market, and finally compares voters' earnings across the two institutions. \autoref{sec:discussion} considers what the findings imply for the study of clientelism and of political markets more broadly, and the Appendix reports robustness checks.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Who Proposes in Clientelist Exchange}\label{sec:literature}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The study of clientelism has long recognized that exchanges are reciprocal \parencite{Auyero2000,Finan2012}, but most quantitative work examines the party side: whom parties and brokers target, how they monitor compliance, and when vote buying is effective \parencite{Stokes2005,Gans-Morse2013,Vicente2014,Leight2020}. A smaller literature studies citizens as the active party. \textcite{Nichter2014a} show that citizens in Brazil and elsewhere routinely request benefits from politicians, and that politicians fulfil many of those requests. \textcite{NichterNunnari2022} study how citizens declare political support to obtain benefits. \textcite{Auerbach2018} document competition among brokers for clients who choose whom to follow, and \textcite{MichaelAuerbach2020} argue that client requests are central to broker--voter relations. On vote selling proper, \textcite{Hicken2015} measure Filipino voters' willingness to sell in the field, and \textcite{Gherghina2026} find that Romanian voters assign high financial value to their votes; see also \textcite{Bahamonde2020a}. Recent work also shows that voters adjust their participation in clientelist exchange to party competitiveness \parencite{Saikkonen2021a,Gherghina2023}.

Measuring vote selling is itself difficult. Survey respondents underreport clientelist exchanges, and list experiments and other indirect techniques have shown that the true prevalence of vote buying can be much higher than direct questions suggest \parencite{Gonzalez-Ocantos2012,GonzalezOcantos2014,KiewietDeJonge2015}. Most of these instruments, however, ask whether a respondent was offered something, not whether the respondent asked for something or named a price. The laboratory offers a complementary route: it allows us to observe prices proposed by voters directly, together with the parties' responses, in an environment in which the value of a vote to each party is known.

This evidence establishes that voters can and do propose terms. It does not tell us whether proposing terms changes what voters get, because the settings in which voters initiate differ from those in which parties do---in poverty, monitoring capacity, broker networks, and electoral competition. Our design holds all of these fixed and varies only the price-setting institution.

Formal models of vote buying, in contrast, treat buyers as the price-setters. In \textcite{Groseclose1996}, two lobbies bid sequentially for legislators' votes, and the second mover buys only as many votes as needed to protect its position. In \textcite{Dekel2008}, two parties bid for voters in general elections, and the party with the higher willingness to pay wins at a price set by its rival's willingness to pay. \textcite{MorganVardy2011} characterize how costly it is to buy a voting body as a function of its size and members' preferences, and \textcite{DalBo2007} shows that a briber who can condition payments on pivotality can control a committee's decision at very low cost, because non-pivotal votes need not be paid. Two ingredients of these models are central to ours: competition between buyers, and the dependence of a vote's value on pivotality. What these models do not consider is the mirror-image institution in which the seller names the price. Our model adds that institution in the simplest environment in which the comparison is meaningful: one voter, two parties with a common budget, and a vote whose value depends on pivotality. The distributive-politics literature on core and swing voters \parencite{Cox1986,Lindbeck1987,Dixit1996,Stokes2005} is also cast in buyer terms; in our model the core-voter logic reappears as a statement about which party wins the voter and how much it pays.

Whether this assumption matters is a question about bargaining institutions. A general lesson of noncooperative bargaining theory is that the rules of bargaining---who proposes, who responds, and whether responders compete---determines how a surplus is divided. In legislative bargaining, the agenda setter extracts rents from coalition partners; in auctions, the seller's ability to set a reserve price or to run a take-it-or-leave-it offer determines how much of the buyers' valuation it captures. The clientelism literature has absorbed this lesson only partially. It recognizes that competition between parties raises the price of votes and that monitoring capacity shapes what parties are willing to pay \parencite{Stokes2005,Gans-Morse2013}, but it rarely asks how the identity of the proposer shapes the price. Our model makes the price-setting institution explicit and shows that it matters most for strongly attached voters, the group that party-centered theories regard as the cheapest to buy.

Laboratory evidence on bargaining suggests why behavior may depart from these predictions. The price-setting-voter game has the structure of an ultimatum game with two responders whose acceptances the proposer can play off against each other. Laboratory ultimatum games show that responders reject positive offers and that proposers anticipate rejection by offering responders far more than the subgame-perfect minimum \parencite{Guth1982,Camerer2003}; competition among responders or proposers moves outcomes toward the equilibrium prediction \parencite{Roth1991}. Quantal response equilibrium formalizes such deviations as payoff-sensitive noise \parencite{McKelvey1995}. On the buyer side, prospect theory \parencite{Kahneman1979,Tversky1992a,Levy1992a} has been used to explain why parties' vote-buying offers depart from expected-utility benchmarks \parencite{Bahamonde2022b}. We use these literatures not as assumptions of the model but as candidate explanations for deviations from it, and we derive the testable signature of each.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{A Model of Price-Setting Power in Clientelist Exchange}\label{sec:model}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Environment}

Two parties, $A$ and $B$, compete in an election, and one voter $j$ can sell a vote. The voter's earnings depend on which party wins, not on how the voter votes: if party $i$ wins and implements policy $\gamma_i$, the voter earns ideological utility $u_i \equiv u_j(\gamma_i) = D - \kappa\,|x_j - \gamma_i|$, where $x_j$ is the voter's ideal point and $D,\kappa>0$. Let $i^\ast = \arg\max_i u_i$ be the voter's preferred party and
\begin{equation}
\Delta \equiv u_{i^\ast} - u_{-i^\ast} \geq 0
\end{equation}
the voter's ideological attachment to it. A transfer $t$ adds to the voter's earnings, so the voter's payoff is $u_{\text{winner}} + t$.

The rest of the electorate is fixed. A set of committed voters supports each party, and the voter's vote is counted, by default, for a party $\ell \in \{A,B\}$ (the voter's ``label''). Let $n_A$ and $n_B$ denote the parties' vote totals when the voter votes by default; the total is odd, so there are no ties. The voter is \emph{pivotal}, $P=1$, if moving the vote from $\ell$ to $-\ell$ changes the winner, that is, if $n_\ell - n_{-\ell} = 1$; otherwise $P=0$. Note that the default label need not coincide with the preferred party: $\ell$ determines which party the vote counts for absent a transaction, while $i^\ast$ determines which outcome the voter prefers.

Each party values office at $W>0$ and has a budget $B_i>0$ from which it can pay transfers. A party's payoff is $W\cdot\mathbb{1}\{\text{wins}\} + B_i - t_i$, where $t_i$ is the transfer it actually pays. A transaction binds: if the voter takes party $i$'s offer or accepted price, the vote is cast for $i$ and $i$ pays. All parameters are common knowledge.

\subsection{The value of a vote and the irrelevance of loss aversion}

Following prospect-theoretic accounts of vote buying, let party $i$'s electoral stake in securing the voter's vote be $R_i = q_i W$, where $q_i$ is the probability that securing the vote changes the outcome in $i$'s favor, and let parties evaluate that stake relative to a reference point. With loss-aversion parameter $\lambda_i \geq 1$ and loss salience $s_i\in[0,1]$, the perceived stake is
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{R}_i = \left[1 + (\lambda_i - 1)\,s_i\right] R_i ,
\end{equation}
and the \emph{effective willingness to pay} is $\overline{R}_i = \min\{B_i, \widetilde{R}_i\}$. The case $\lambda_i=1$ is the expected-payoff benchmark.

\begin{lemma}[Value of a vote]\label{lem:value}
In this environment $q_A = q_B = P$. Hence $R_A = R_B = P\cdot W$: a vote is worth the full value of office to either party when the voter is pivotal and nothing otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $P=0$, no allocation of the voter's vote changes the winner, so securing it is worth nothing. If $P=1$, the party that secures the vote wins and the other loses; securing the vote is worth $W$ to $\ell$ (it protects a victory) and to $-\ell$ (it creates one).
\end{proof}

\autoref{lem:value} has a direct implication for measurement. In equilibrium, a party's vote share affects the value of the vote only through pivotality: what matters is whether the voter can change the outcome. Any further effect of vote share on offers must therefore come from how parties perceive electoral risk, which is where reference-dependent preferences enter.

\begin{lemma}[Irrelevance of loss aversion when budgets bind]\label{lem:loss}
If $B_i \leq W$, then $\overline{R}_i = P\cdot B_i$ for every $\lambda_i \geq 1$ and $s_i \in [0,1]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $P=0$, $\widetilde{R}_i = 0$ and $\overline{R}_i = 0$. If $P=1$, $\widetilde{R}_i \geq W \geq B_i$, so $\overline{R}_i = B_i$.
\end{proof}

\autoref{lem:loss} locates where loss aversion can matter. When parties hold correct beliefs about pivotality and budgets are smaller than the value of office---as in our experiment, where budgets range from 900 to 1,800 points and office is worth 2,400---loss aversion cannot raise willingness to pay above what the budget already allows, and it cannot create willingness to pay for a vote that does not matter. Any effect of loss aversion must therefore operate through beliefs about pivotality. We take up this channel in \autoref{sec:behavioral}. In what follows we set $B_A = B_B = M < W$, as in the experiment, so that $\overline{R}_A = \overline{R}_B = P\cdot M$.

\subsection{Price-setting parties}

In the price-setting-parties game, $\Gamma_P$, both parties simultaneously post offers $s_A, s_B \in [0, M]$. The voter observes both offers and takes one of them or neither; taking party $i$'s offer casts the vote for $i$ and triggers the transfer $s_i$. Ties are broken in favor of the voter's preferred party.

\begin{proposition}[Price-setting parties]\label{prop:buying}
Suppose $P=1$. In the equilibrium of $\Gamma_P$ in which no party offers more than its willingness to pay, the preferred party $i^\ast$ secures the vote. If $\Delta < M$, it pays $M-\Delta$ and the rival offers $M$; if $\Delta \geq M$, it pays nothing (or an arbitrarily small amount, if the vote must be moved). The voter earns
\[
U^{P} = u_{i^\ast} + \max\{0,\, M - \Delta\}.
\]
If $P=0$, no party makes a positive offer and the voter earns the ideological payoff of the default winner.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
With $P=1$ each party is willing to pay up to $M$ to secure the vote (\autoref{lem:loss}). The voter takes the offer that maximizes $u_i + s_i$. The rival $-i^\ast$ can attract the voter only by offering more than $\Delta$ above $i^\ast$'s offer; since it will offer at most $M$, $i^\ast$ secures the vote by offering $\max\{0, M - \Delta\}$, and the rival cannot profitably outbid it. This is the standard outcome of price competition between two buyers with equal valuations when the seller prefers one of them \parencite[compare][]{Dekel2008}. With $P=0$, any positive transfer is a pure loss.
\end{proof}

\autoref{prop:buying} recovers the core-voter intuition in equilibrium form: when parties set prices, the vote goes to the party the voter already prefers, and that party pays less the stronger the voter's attachment. It is cheaper to buy a supporter than an opponent because the rival must overcome $\Delta$. Note, however, that the transfer is determined by the rival's willingness to pay, not by the minimum compensation the voter would require in isolation.

\subsection{Price-setting voter}

In the price-setting-voter game, $\Gamma_V$, the voter first posts prices $a_A, a_B \in [0,M]$, one for each party. The parties then simultaneously accept or reject the price addressed to them. Finally, the voter takes one of the accepted prices or none; taking party $i$'s price casts the vote for $i$ and triggers the transfer $a_i$. Parties accept when indifferent. Bargaining here is structural. Players never communicate; every move is a posted price or a decision to accept or reject one.

\begin{proposition}[Price-setting voter]\label{prop:selling}
Suppose $P=1$. If $\Delta < M$ or $\ell \neq i^\ast$, there is a subgame-perfect equilibrium in which the voter requests $a_A = a_B = M$, both parties accept, and the voter sells to $i^\ast$, earning
\[
U^{V} = u_{i^\ast} + M .
\]
If $\Delta \geq M$ and $\ell = i^\ast$, the voter's threat to sell to the rival is not credible, the preferred party rejects any positive price, and the voter earns $u_{i^\ast}$. If $P=0$, both parties reject any positive price and the voter earns the ideological payoff of the default winner.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Consider $P=1$. For the non-label party $-\ell$, accepting any $a_{-\ell}\leq M$ is weakly dominant: if the voter takes the price, $-\ell$ wins and earns $W + M - a_{-\ell} > M$; otherwise its payoff is $M$ whatever it does. For the label party, accepting matters only if the voter would otherwise sell to $-\ell$. Suppose first $\ell \neq i^\ast$. The voter requests $M$ from $i^\ast = -\ell$, which accepts; since $u_{i^\ast} + M$ exceeds anything $\ell$ can offer the voter ($u_{\ell} + a_\ell \leq u_{-i^\ast} + M$), the voter sells to $i^\ast$ at $M$. Suppose next $\ell = i^\ast$ and $\Delta < M$. Then $u_{-i^\ast} + M > u_{i^\ast}$, so the voter's threat to sell to the rival at $M$ is credible; the preferred party prefers paying $M$ and winning ($W$) to losing ($M$), accepts $a_{i^\ast} = M$, and the voter sells to it. Finally, if $\ell = i^\ast$ and $\Delta \geq M$, the voter would not sell to the rival even at $M$, so $i^\ast$ wins by rejecting. With $P=0$, a party that accepts pays only if chosen and gains nothing from being chosen, so accepting a positive price is weakly dominated.
\end{proof}

The price-setting-voter game is an ultimatum game in which the voter is the proposer and can use one responder's acceptance as a threat against the other. As in the ultimatum game, the proposer extracts the whole surplus available, which here is the parties' willingness to pay, $M$. Note that rejection never occurs in equilibrium: a voter who knows the parties' willingness to pay never asks for more. It is sometimes argued that voter-initiated exchange exposes voters to rejection risk; \autoref{prop:selling} shows that this cannot be an equilibrium property of the game, so if rejection occurs, it is a behavioral phenomenon (\autoref{sec:behavioral}).

\subsection{The proposer advantage}

Comparing Propositions \ref{prop:buying} and \ref{prop:selling} yields the paper's central prediction.

\begin{proposition}[Proposer advantage]\label{prop:advantage}
When the voter is pivotal, moving price-setting power from the parties to the voter raises the voter's earnings by
\[
U^{V} - U^{P} =
\begin{cases}
\min\{\Delta, M\} & \text{if } \Delta < M \text{ or } \ell \neq i^\ast,\\
0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\]
and lowers the winning party's earnings by the same amount. When the voter is not pivotal, the institution makes no difference. Under both institutions, the preferred party wins the vote.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Subtract $U^{P}$ in \autoref{prop:buying} from $U^{V}$ in \autoref{prop:selling}. Transfers are zero-sum between the voter and the winning party, and the losing party pays nothing in either game.
\end{proof}

The intuition is simple. When parties set prices, they compete for the voter, but the competition is softened by the voter's ideological attachment: the preferred party needs only to match its rival's best offer net of $\Delta$, and keeps $\Delta$ of the budget. When the voter sets prices, the voter can demand the whole budget from the preferred party, because the rival's willingness to accept makes the threat to defect credible. The voter's ideological attachment, which is a handicap when parties compete for the vote, becomes irrelevant when the voter sets the price. The proposer advantage is therefore largest for voters with strong ideological attachments---exactly the ``core'' voters whom party-centered theories expect to be cheapest to buy.

\autoref{prop:advantage} runs against a natural behavioral conjecture, namely that voters should earn more when parties initiate the exchange, because loss-averse parties would pay an ``insurance premium.'' Under correct beliefs and binding budgets, there is no room for such a premium (\autoref{lem:loss}), and the voter's power as a proposer dominates.

\subsection{Earnings and efficiency}

Because transfers move points from parties to the voter without creating or destroying any, the two institutions generate the same total surplus in equilibrium: the preferred party wins in both, and the sum of the voter's and the parties' earnings is the same. The institution affects only the division of the surplus. The same is true of the behavioral deviations we document below. A party that pays for a non-pivotal vote transfers points to the voter without changing the outcome or the total. A party that rejects an acceptable price from a pivotal voter may change the outcome; in that case the total changes only through the voter's ideological payoff, by at most $\Delta$, because one party earns $W$ in any case. This feature of the design is useful: it means that the comparison of voter earnings across institutions is a direct measure of how the institution, and deviations from equilibrium play under each institution, redistribute a fixed surplus.

\subsection{A numerical illustration}

To fix ideas, consider a round with a budget of $M=1{,}300$ points and a pivotal voter who earns $u_A = 1{,}800$ points if Party A wins and $u_B = 1{,}300$ if Party B wins, so that $i^\ast = A$ and $\Delta = 500$. With price-setting parties, Party B is willing to offer its entire budget to win, which would give the voter $1{,}300 + 1{,}300 = 2{,}600$ points. To keep the voter, Party A must match this, which requires an offer of $800$: the voter then earns $1{,}800 + 800 = 2{,}600$, Party A wins and keeps $500$ of its budget, and Party B pays nothing. With a price-setting voter, the voter asks both parties for $1{,}300$. Party B accepts, because winning at that price is better than losing; given that acceptance, Party A also accepts, because paying $1{,}300$ and winning is better than losing. The voter sells to Party A and earns $1{,}800 + 1{,}300 = 3{,}100$ points. The proposer advantage is $500 = \Delta$. If instead the voter were indifferent between the parties ($\Delta = 0$), the competition between buyers would already give the voter the whole budget and the institution would not matter. If the voter were strongly attached to Party A ($\Delta \geq 1{,}300$), Party B could not compete at any price, and with price-setting parties the voter would receive nothing; with a price-setting voter, the outcome depends on whether the voter's default vote counts for Party A (no transfer) or Party B (the voter extracts $1{,}300$ from Party A).

\subsection{Extensions and comparative statics}

Three features of the model deserve comment. First, the assumption of common budgets is not essential. With budgets $B_A \neq B_B$, both below $W$, Propositions \ref{prop:buying} and \ref{prop:selling} generalize directly: with price-setting parties the voter earns the larger of $u_{i^\ast}$ and $u_{-i^\ast} + B_{-i^\ast}$ (the rival's best offer), while with a price-setting voter the voter earns $\max_i\{u_i + B_i\}$ whenever the threat is credible. The proposer advantage is then the difference between the voter's best ``bid-adjusted'' value and the second-best one, which is positive whenever the voter is not indifferent. Second, the model has only one voter who can sell a vote. With several sellers, competition among them would weaken the bargaining position of a price-setting voter, just as competition among buyers strengthens the voter when parties set prices; our prediction is therefore an upper bound on the proposer advantage in larger electorates. Third, the proposer advantage depends on the voter's ideological attachment in a specific way. With price-setting parties, the transfer to a pivotal voter, $\max\{0, M - \Delta\}$, falls one-for-one with $\Delta$: strongly attached (``core'') voters receive less, because the rival party cannot compete for them. With a price-setting voter, the transfer is $M$ regardless of $\Delta$ (whenever the threat is credible). The institution thus flips the relationship between attachment and transfers, and the proposer advantage should rise one-for-one with $\Delta$ up to the budget. This comparative static provides a second, sharper test of the model, which we use in \autoref{sec:results}.

\subsection{Behavioral benchmarks}\label{sec:behavioral}

The equilibrium benchmark makes sharp predictions, several of which our data reject. To interpret deviations, we consider two departures from the benchmark that keep the structure of the game but relax the assumption that players best respond to correct beliefs. Each generates a testable signature.

\paragraph{Perceived pivotality and loss aversion.} Suppose parties believe that a non-pivotal vote decides the election with probability $\theta \in (0,1)$, for instance because they do not fully process the electorate or because they overweight small probabilities \parencite{Tversky1992a}. Then $R_i = \theta W$ when $P=0$, and
\begin{equation}
\overline{R}_i^{\,P=0} = \min\left\{ M,\ \left[1 + (\lambda_i - 1)s_i\right]\theta W \right\}.
\end{equation}
Whenever $\theta W < M$, loss aversion now has bite: parties will pay positive amounts for non-pivotal votes, and the amount rises with $\lambda_i$ and $s_i$. Misperceived pivotality and loss aversion are thus complements: the first creates the room in which the second operates. This yields three signatures. (B1) Parties make offers to, and accept requests from, non-pivotal voters. (B2) Because willingness to pay is interior and heterogeneous across parties, acceptance of non-pivotal requests falls with the price requested. (B3) If loss salience is higher for the party that leads before the voter acts---for which failing to secure the vote is framed as losing a race it is winning---than for the trailing party, then leading parties offer more and accept more than trailing parties. Signatures B1 and B2 follow from mistaken beliefs alone; B3 is specific to loss aversion.

\paragraph{Noisy responders and rejection risk.} Suppose that parties accept a price $a \leq \overline{R}_i$ only with probability $1-r(a)$, where the rejection probability $r$ is increasing in $a$, as in quantal response models in which errors are more likely when they are less costly to the responder \parencite{McKelvey1995}, or in models of ultimatum responders who reject offers they consider unfair \parencite{Camerer2003}. A voter who anticipates this chooses a price that trades off the size of the transfer against the risk of rejection.

\begin{remark}[Optimal request under rejection risk]\label{rem:noise}
If the voter maximizes the expected transfer $a\,[1-r(a)]$ on $[0,M]$ and $r$ is differentiable and increasing, the optimal request is strictly below $M$ whenever $M\, r'(M) > 1 - r(M)$. For example, with logit acceptance, $1-r(a) = [1+\exp((a-M)/\mu)]^{-1}$, the condition holds whenever the noise parameter satisfies $\mu < M/2$.
\end{remark}

Under rejection risk, two further signatures follow. (B4) Parties reject some pivotal requests below the budget. (B5) Pivotal voters request less than the budget. As $\mu \to 0$, rejection disappears and the request converges to $M$, recovering \autoref{prop:selling}. This formalizes the original intuition that voter initiative carries rejection risk, but as a departure from equilibrium play rather than a feature of it. Together, B1 and B5 imply that the realized proposer advantage should be smaller than \autoref{prop:advantage} predicts: overpayment raises voter earnings when parties set prices, while cautious requests lower them when the voter does.

\autoref{tab:predictions} summarizes the benchmark predictions and the behavioral signatures that we test.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Experimental Design}\label{sec:design}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Implementation and sample}

The experiment was programmed in oTree \parencite{Chen2016oTree} and conducted in a laboratory in \redacted{Santiago, Chile}, administered jointly by \redacted{the University of Santiago and the Oxford-Nuffield Centre for Experimental Social Sciences}, between \redacted{April 20 and May 28, 2021}.\footnote{Participants were recruited in the \redacted{university district} and received a show-up fee of \redacted{2,000 CLP} (approximately \redacted{2.1 euros}) plus their earnings in experimental points, converted into \redacted{pesos} at a fixed exchange rate announced in advance.} Each session was assigned to one institution: \Sexpr{sVB} sessions played the price-setting-parties game and \Sexpr{sVS} sessions played the price-setting-voter game. The institution therefore varies between sessions, and we cluster standard errors by session whenever we compare institutions. The analysis sample comprises \Sexpr{nAll} participants (\Sexpr{nVB} with price-setting parties and \Sexpr{nVS} with a price-setting voter); \Sexpr{rt$n.dropped} participant records with zero recorded earnings were excluded.\footnote{The raw oTree export contains \Sexpr{rt$n.raw} participant records. \autoref{tab:flow} in the Appendix reports the flow from records to analyzed observations.} After two practice rounds, participants played three paid rounds in fixed groups of three, with roles reassigned at random within the group each round. Each round thus produces one voter decision and two party decisions: \Sexpr{vrVB} voter-rounds and \Sexpr{2*vrVB} party-rounds with price-setting parties, and \Sexpr{vrVS} voter-rounds and \Sexpr{2*vrVS} party-rounds with a price-setting voter.

\subsection{The game}

In each round, one participant was the voter and two were Party A and Party B. \autoref{fig:flow} shows the sequence of moves. The environment maps directly onto the model.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/Users/hectorbahamonde/research/Exp_Vote_Selling/experimental_flow_3.pdf}
  \caption{Sequence of moves in the two institutions. The institution was assigned at the session level; all other features were redrawn each round.}
  \label{fig:flow}
\end{figure}

In each round, the voter was told how many points they would earn if Party A or Party B won: $u_i = 2{,}000 - 20\,|x_j - \gamma_i|$, with $\gamma_A$ drawn from $\{1,\dots,50\}$, $\gamma_B$ from $\{51,\dots,100\}$, and $x_j$ from $\{1,\dots,100\}$. These induced payoffs define the voter's preferred party and the attachment $\Delta$. The voter's earnings depended on which party won, not on how the voter voted.

Both parties received the same budget $M$, drawn uniformly between 900 and 1,800 points, and earned $W = 2{,}400$ points if they won the election, in addition to the unspent budget. A party paid a transfer only if the voter took its offer (or its accepted price). Because $M < W$ in every round, \autoref{lem:loss} applies throughout.

Pivotality was induced through a fictional electorate. An electorate of three or five votes was displayed, split between the parties; the voter's vote was counted for the label party unless the voter took the other party's offer. The label was displayed on screen and need not coincide with the party favored by the voter's induced payoffs: it did so in \Sexpr{f0(lcVB)} percent of voter-rounds with price-setting parties and \Sexpr{f0(lcVS)} percent with a price-setting voter. Throughout, we define the preferred party and $\Delta$ from the induced payoffs; Propositions \ref{prop:buying}--\ref{prop:advantage} hold for either relationship between the label and the preferred party.

Because the label and the preferred party vary across rounds, the design separates two quantities that observational data usually confound: the party a voter would support absent any exchange, and the party the voter prefers once the exchange is possible. This separation matters for the model. When the label party is also the preferred party, a price-setting voter can obtain a payment from it only because the rival's acceptance gives the voter somewhere else to sell, and that alternative is attractive only if the budget exceeds the voter's attachment. When the label party is the rival, the preferred party must pay to move the vote, and the voter's request to it is credible whatever the attachment. The variation therefore exposes both branches of \autoref{prop:selling} to observation. It also ensures that pivotality, which depends on the label and the displayed electorate, is not mechanically tied to the voter's ideological payoffs, so that the comparison between pivotal and non-pivotal rounds is not a comparison between more and less attached voters. The voter was pivotal when the label party led by exactly one vote (2--1 or 3--2), so that moving the vote would reverse the outcome. In all other configurations the outcome could not be changed. All information was public.

The two institutions differed in the sequence of moves. With price-setting parties, each party chose an offer between zero and the budget without observing the other's offer; the voter then took one offer or none. With a price-setting voter, the voter chose a price between zero and the budget for each party; each party, observing both prices, accepted or rejected the one addressed to it; the voter then took one accepted price or none. Under both institutions, taking an offer cast the vote for that party, so the commitment problem that characterizes real clientelism is absent by design.

Decision screens displayed the budget, the voter's points under each possible winner, and the fictional electorate, including the vote count before the voter acted. Parties deciding whether to accept a price in the price-setting-voter game saw both prices the voter had posted, so they knew whether the rival had been offered a better or worse deal. Voters choosing among offers saw the amounts offered (or, with a price-setting voter, which parties had accepted their prices). Each screen had a limit of 240 seconds; across all participants and rounds, the data record 16 screens that timed out.

The two institutions are designed to differ only in who proposes the price, but a change in the proposer necessarily changes some other features of the game, and it is important to be explicit about them. First, the action spaces differ: with price-setting parties, each party chooses a number and the voter chooses among offers; with a price-setting voter, the voter chooses two numbers and each party makes a binary decision. Second, the information at the moment of commitment differs: a party that accepts a price knows exactly what it will pay if chosen, whereas a party that posts an offer does not know whether its rival's offer will be higher. Third, the price-setting voter can tailor different prices to the two parties, while each party's offer is a single number. These differences are not confounds in the usual sense, because they are part of what it means for one side rather than the other to set the price; the model incorporates all three, and its predictions account for them. But they do mean that our comparison identifies the effect of the institution as a whole, not of the order of moves holding the decision problem fixed. We therefore describe the treatments as price-setting institutions \parencite{Smith1982}, rather than as differences in ``initiative.''

\subsection{Empirical strategy}

Because the benchmark makes point predictions for each round given its draws, we test it by comparing observed behavior with the benchmark computed round by round. For each voter-round, we compute the voter's equilibrium earnings under the institution actually in place and under the alternative institution, using the round's budget, induced payoffs, label, and electorate. For each party-round, we compute the equilibrium offer (with price-setting parties) or the equilibrium acceptance decision (with a price-setting voter). Averages of these benchmarks across rounds give the predicted values in \autoref{tab:results}; because the draws differ across rounds, the benchmark differs slightly across institutions and across groups of voters.

Our tests are of three kinds, each tied to a simple estimating equation. The first asks, within each institution, whether behavior responds to pivotality as the benchmark predicts. For a decision $d$ in round $r$, we estimate
\begin{equation}\label{eq:within}
y_{dr} = \beta_0 + \beta_1\, \mathit{Pivotal}_{dr} + \varepsilon_{dr},
\end{equation}
where $y_{dr}$ is a party's offer as a percentage of the budget, an indicator (scaled to 100) for making a positive offer, or an indicator (scaled to 100) for accepting a voter's request, and $\mathit{Pivotal}_{dr}$ equals one when the voter can change the outcome. The coefficient $\beta_1$ is the difference in behavior between pivotal and non-pivotal voters, which the benchmark predicts to be large: parties should treat a pivotal vote as worth their whole budget and a non-pivotal vote as worth nothing. We fit \autoref{eq:within} as a linear probability or linear model and cluster standard errors on the decision-maker.\footnote{With a single binary regressor, the linear probability model reproduces the difference in proportions exactly.} When we compare a single mean with its benchmark (for example, the share of parties that make an offer to a non-pivotal voter, which the benchmark puts at zero), we use the same model with the intercept only.

The second test is the central one. It compares voters' earnings across the two institutions. For voter $v$ in round $r$ of session $s$, we estimate
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gap}
\mathit{Earnings}_{vrs} = \alpha + \tau\, \mathit{VoterSets}_{s} + \varepsilon_{vrs},
\end{equation}
where $\mathit{VoterSets}_{s}$ equals one in sessions with a price-setting voter and zero in sessions with price-setting parties. The coefficient $\tau$ is the realized proposer advantage. Its benchmark, $\tau^\ast$, is the mean equilibrium earnings of voters in price-setting-voter sessions minus the mean equilibrium earnings of voters in price-setting-parties sessions, each computed from that session's own draws. We estimate \autoref{eq:gap} separately for pivotal and non-pivotal voters and test two hypotheses: that the institutions do not differ ($\tau = 0$), and that the realized advantage equals its benchmark ($\tau = \tau^\ast$). Because the institution was assigned by session, we cluster standard errors by session and, to account for the small number of clusters $G$, base inference on the $t$ distribution with $G-2$ degrees of freedom. Two further quantities help interpret a small $\hat\tau$: the minimum detectable effect at 80 percent power, $(t_{0.975,\,G-2} + t_{0.80,\,G-2})\,\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat\tau)$, and the smallest symmetric bound within which an equivalence test \parencite{Lakens2017} declares the two institutions equivalent, $|\hat\tau| + t_{0.95,\,G-2}\,\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat\tau)$. We also compute a randomization-inference $p$-value for $\tau = 0$ by permuting the institution across sessions.

The third test examines the shape of the proposer advantage. The model predicts that the advantage of a pivotal voter rises one-for-one with the voter's ideological attachment $\Delta$ (as long as $\Delta$ is below the budget). Among pivotal voters, we therefore estimate
\begin{equation}\label{eq:slope}
\mathit{Earnings}_{vrs} = \alpha + \tau\, \mathit{VoterSets}_{s} + \eta\, \Delta_{vr} + \omega\, (\mathit{VoterSets}_{s} \times \Delta_{vr}) + \varepsilon_{vrs},
\end{equation}
again clustering by session. The benchmark predicts $\omega = 1$; if price-setting power made no difference, $\omega$ would be zero.

We compute each voter's earnings in a round from the rules of the game: the induced payoff from the winning party plus the transfer taken. Results using the points recorded by the software lead to the same conclusions (\autoref{tab:gaps} in the Appendix).

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We report means with 95 percent confidence intervals clustered on the decision-maker (the party participant for party decisions; the voter for voter decisions) when describing behavior within an institution, and clustered by session when comparing institutions. \autoref{tab:results} collects the main benchmark comparisons, and Figures \ref{fig:behaviour} and \ref{fig:payoffs} display them.

