\subsection{Pivotality moves behavior in the predicted direction}

The benchmark's first-order prediction is that parties should care only about whether the voter is pivotal (\autoref{lem:value}). Behavior responds to pivotality under both institutions. With price-setting parties, \Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.p.any)} percent of parties facing a pivotal voter make a positive offer, against \Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.np.any$est)} percent facing a non-pivotal voter (difference \Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.piv.any$est)} percentage points, $p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$vb.piv.any$p)}), and offers are \Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.piv$est)} percentage points of the budget larger ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$vb.piv$p)}). With a price-setting voter, parties accept \Sexpr{f1(acc1)} percent of positive requests from pivotal voters and \Sexpr{f1(acc0)} percent from non-pivotal voters (difference \Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.acc.test$est)} points, $p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$vs.acc.test$p)}). The direction is right in both games; the magnitudes, however, are far from the benchmark's all-or-nothing prediction, as Panels A and B of \autoref{fig:behaviour} show.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/Users/hectorbahamonde/research/Exp_Vote_Selling/benchmark_behaviour.pdf}
  \caption{Party and voter behavior against the equilibrium benchmark. Panel A: parties' offers as a percentage of the budget (price-setting parties); the pivotal benchmark averages the preferred party's offer ($M-\Delta$) and the rival's ($M$). Panel B: share of positive requests accepted (price-setting voter). Panel C: pivotal voters' requests as a percentage of the budget. Means with 95\% confidence intervals clustered by party participant (A, B) or voter (C).}
  \label{fig:behaviour}
\end{figure}

\autoref{prop:buying} also predicts which party wins a pivotal voter and how much each party offers. The direction of the predicted asymmetry between the parties appears in the data: the preferred party offers \Sexpr{f1(vbP.pref)} percent of the budget and its rival \Sexpr{f1(vbP.disp)} percent, but the difference is small and imprecise (\Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.pref.test$est)} points, $p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$vb.pref.test$p)}), and the rival offers less than half of what the benchmark predicts. As a result the preferred party secures the pivotal vote in \Sexpr{f0(pv(VB,1,"win.pref"))} percent of rounds rather than in all of them. On average, however, the transfer that a pivotal voter receives when parties set prices is close to the benchmark (\Sexpr{f0(pv(VB,1,"transfer"))} against \Sexpr{f0(pv(VB,1,"bench.transfer"))} points), even though the offers that produce it are not: both parties offer less than the benchmark, and in the remaining \Sexpr{f0(100 - pv(VB,1,"win.pref"))} percent of rounds the voter sells to the rival.

\subsection{Parties pay for votes that cannot change the outcome}

The clearest deviation from the benchmark concerns non-pivotal voters (E1). A non-pivotal vote is worth nothing to either party, yet half of the parties facing a non-pivotal voter in the price-setting-parties game make a positive offer (\Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.np.any$est)} percent, 95\% CI \Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.np.any$lo)}--\Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.np.any$hi)}), and positive offers average \Sexpr{f1(rt$vb.np.pos$est)} percent of the budget. Non-pivotal offers are therefore not small gestures: they are about as large, conditional on being made, as offers to pivotal voters. In the price-setting-voter game, parties accept \Sexpr{f1(acc0)} percent of positive requests from non-pivotal voters, although these requests average \Sexpr{f1(req0)} percent of the budget. Non-pivotal voters receive on average \Sexpr{f0(pv(VB,0,"transfer"))} points per round when parties set prices and \Sexpr{f0(pv(VS,0,"transfer"))} points when they set prices themselves; under both institutions they earn several hundred points more than the benchmark (\autoref{fig:payoffs}, right panel). Signature B1 is thus strongly present.

Signature B2 is also present: in the price-setting-voter game, the probability that a party accepts a non-pivotal request falls by \Sexpr{f2(abs(rt$vs.acc.price.np$est))} percentage points for each additional percentage point of the budget requested ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$vs.acc.price.np$p)}). Parties thus behave as if the non-pivotal vote had some positive but limited value, as perceived pivotality implies, rather than accepting or rejecting at random.

Signature B3 asks whether the party's position in the race shapes this overpayment. With price-setting parties, leading parties facing a non-pivotal voter offer \Sexpr{f1(abs(rt$lead.vb$est))} percentage points of the budget \emph{less} than trailing parties ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$lead.vb$p)}); among pivotal voters, the difference is also negative (\Sexpr{f1(rt$lead.vb.p$est)} points, $p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$lead.vb.p$p)}). With a price-setting voter, leading parties accept non-pivotal requests \Sexpr{f1(rt$lead.vs$est)} points more often than trailing parties ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$lead.vs$p)}; \autoref{tab:lead} in the Appendix). These differences are imprecisely estimated and differ in sign across the two institutions, so our sample cannot pin down how the race position modulates overpayment. What the data show clearly is that parties attach value to votes that cannot change the outcome (B1 and B2), the channel through which loss aversion operates in the model.

\subsection{Voters under-use their power to set prices}

The benchmark predicts that a pivotal voter who sets prices should request the full budget from both parties and receive it (E3). Pivotal voters request on average \Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.p.req$est)} percent of the budget (95\% CI \Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.p.req$lo)}--\Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.p.req$hi)}; Panel C of \autoref{fig:behaviour}), and only \Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.p.full$est)} percent of their requests equal the budget. Many request nothing from one of the parties: \Sexpr{f0(vsZ.disp)} percent of requests to the party the voter's payoffs disfavor, and \Sexpr{f0(vsZ.pref)} percent to the favored party, are zero, which forgoes the threat that gives the price-setting voter its power. Requests from pivotal and non-pivotal voters are similar in size (\Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.p.req$est)} and \Sexpr{f1(rt$vs.np.req$est)} percent of the budget), so voters do not price their pivotality.

Some caution is warranted from the voter's point of view, because parties reject requests that the benchmark says they should accept (B4): \Sexpr{f1(rej1)} percent of positive requests from pivotal voters were rejected, although every request was within the budget and the vote was worth more than the budget to the party. The acceptance rate among pivotal requests does not decline significantly with the price requested ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$vs.acc.price.p$p)}), but the number of such requests is small (\Sexpr{nacc1}). Voters who set prices thus behave like ultimatum proposers who expect responders to reject greedy demands (B5), and the result is a transfer well below the benchmark: pivotal voters receive \Sexpr{f0(pv(VS,1,"transfer"))} points on average, against a benchmark of \Sexpr{f0(pv(VS,1,"bench.transfer"))}. The preferred party secures the pivotal vote in only \Sexpr{f0(pv(VS,1,"win.pref"))} percent of rounds; in the remaining rounds the vote goes to the rival, an outcome the benchmark rules out.

\subsection{The proposer advantage disappears}

\autoref{prop:advantage} predicts that a pivotal voter earns substantially more as a price-setter. Evaluated at each round's draws, the advantage averages \Sexpr{f0(rt$pred.gap.piv)} points across pivotal voter-rounds. Because the two institutions were played in different sessions, with different draws, the benchmark for the observed comparison is the difference between mean equilibrium earnings in the two sets of sessions, $\tau^\ast = \Sexpr{f0(Gr$bench)}$ points. \autoref{fig:payoffs} compares observed and predicted earnings. When parties set prices, pivotal voters earn \Sexpr{f0(pv(VB,1,"rule"))} points, close to the benchmark of \Sexpr{f0(pv(VB,1,"benchmark"))}. When the voter sets prices, pivotal voters earn \Sexpr{f0(pv(VS,1,"rule"))} points, far below the benchmark of \Sexpr{f0(pv(VS,1,"benchmark"))}. The observed proposer advantage, $\hat\tau$ in \autoref{eq:gap}, is \Sexpr{f0(Gr$est)} points (95\% CI \Sexpr{f0(Gr$lo)} to \Sexpr{f0(Gr$hi)}, $p$ = \Sexpr{f2(Gr$p)}), and the benchmark is rejected ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(Gr$p.bench)}).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{/Users/hectorbahamonde/research/Exp_Vote_Selling/benchmark_payoffs.pdf}
  \caption{Voter earnings per round by institution and pivotality: observed (95\% confidence intervals clustered by session) and equilibrium benchmark (Propositions \ref{prop:buying} and \ref{prop:selling}).}
  \label{fig:payoffs}
\end{figure}

This null is informative. With \Sexpr{Gr$G} session clusters, the minimum effect we can detect with 80 percent power at the 5 percent level is \Sexpr{f0(Gr$mde)} points, well below the predicted \Sexpr{f0(Gr$bench)}.\footnote{The experiment comprises \Sexpr{sAll} sessions. One session with price-setting parties produced no pivotal voter-rounds, so tests restricted to pivotal voters use \Sexpr{Gr$G} session clusters, while tests over all voters or over non-pivotal voters use all \Sexpr{Ga$G}.} An equivalence test \parencite{Lakens2017} rejects differences larger than $\pm$\Sexpr{f0(Gr$tost)} points for pivotal voters and $\pm$\Sexpr{f0(Ga$tost)} points across all rounds, where the overall estimate is \Sexpr{f0(Ga$est)} points (95\% CI \Sexpr{f0(Ga$lo)} to \Sexpr{f0(Ga$hi)}). We can therefore rule out a proposer advantage of the size the model predicts, though not a modest one. The parties' side mirrors the voters': parties earn \Sexpr{f0(abs(pgap$estimate))} points less per round with a price-setting voter than with price-setting parties ($p$ = \Sexpr{f2(pgap$p.value)}), against a predicted loss for the winning party equal to the voter's gain.

Two further checks support this conclusion. First, the model predicts not only the size but the shape of the proposer advantage: it should rise one-for-one with the voter's ideological attachment (E5). Among pivotal voters, the estimated slope of the advantage with respect to $\Delta$ ($\omega$ in \autoref{eq:slope}) is \Sexpr{f2(rt$int$est)} (standard error \Sexpr{f2(rt$int$se)}), statistically indistinguishable from zero ($p$ = \Sexpr{f2(rt$int$p)}) and clearly different from the predicted slope of one ($p$ \Sexpr{fp(rt$int$p.one)}). Strongly attached voters, who stand to gain most from setting the price, gain no more than weakly attached ones. Second, randomization inference, which permutes the institution across the \Sexpr{sAll} sessions, gives $p$ = \Sexpr{f2(rt$ri$p)} for the null of no difference among pivotal voters, close to the cluster-robust result.

The decomposition in \autoref{fig:payoffs} shows where the predicted rent goes. For pivotal voters, the price-setting-parties institution performs close to the benchmark, while the price-setting-voter institution falls well short of it because voters ask for too little and parties sometimes refuse. For non-pivotal voters, both institutions deliver several hundred points more than the benchmark (\Sexpr{f0(over.VB)} and \Sexpr{f0(over.VS)} points), because parties pay for votes that do not matter. Price-setting power, which in theory belongs to whoever proposes, is in practice dissipated on one side of the market and given away on the other.

\subsection{What the offsetting pattern implies}

It is worth emphasizing what the offsetting pattern does and does not imply. It does not imply that the institution is irrelevant for everything. The composition of transfers differs: when parties set prices, a larger share of what voters receive comes from payments for votes that do not matter, whereas when the voter sets prices, a larger share comes from payments by the preferred party in pivotal rounds. Nor does the near-equality of average voter earnings imply that each voter is indifferent between the institutions. What it implies is that a voter who could choose the institution before learning whether they would be pivotal would, on average, gain little by insisting on setting the price---the opposite of what the equilibrium analysis suggests.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Who sets the price of a vote should matter. In a simple model of clientelist exchange with two parties and one voter, moving price-setting power from parties to the voter shifts a rent equal to the voter's ideological attachment, capped by the parties' budget, from the winning party to the voter. The model nests vote buying and vote selling as two institutions for the same exchange and shows that the voter's attachment, which softens competition between parties when they set prices, ceases to protect the preferred party when the voter does. In our laboratory implementation, however, the predicted rent does not materialize. Pivotal voters earn about the same under both institutions, and the benchmark difference is rejected with a well-powered test. Two regularities explain why. Parties pay for votes that cannot change the outcome, which raises voters' earnings when parties set prices; and voters who set prices ask for about half of what parties would pay, which lowers their earnings when they hold the power to propose.

These findings speak to three debates. First, they qualify the expectation, implicit in work that emphasizes citizens' agency in clientelism \parencite{Nichter2014a,NichterNunnari2022}, that voter-initiated exchange benefits voters. The formal right to name the price is not the same as the ability to use it: in our data, voters who set prices behave like cautious ultimatum proposers, and the parties they face reject some requests that it would pay them to accept. Whether the same is true in the field, where requests are embedded in repeated relationships with brokers, is an open question, but our results suggest that the distribution of clientelist rents should not be inferred from who initiates the exchange.

Second, the results bear on theories of vote buying that put pivotality at the center \parencite{Groseclose1996,DalBo2007,Dekel2008}. Our participants understood that pivotal votes are worth more---offers and acceptances rise sharply with pivotality---but they did not treat non-pivotal votes as worthless. If buyers in real elections, who face far more uncertainty about pivotality than our participants, behave similarly, vote buying will be more widespread and less efficiently targeted than models with fully rational buyers predict. This is consistent with the observation that clientelist parties distribute benefits well beyond the voters whose behavior they could plausibly change.

Third, the results specify the channel through which prospect theory shapes clientelist exchange. When budgets bind and beliefs are correct, loss aversion has no observable implication for pivotal votes (\autoref{lem:loss}). It matters when parties overestimate their chances of being decisive, as the parties in our experiment appear to do: they pay for votes that cannot change the outcome. Future designs that vary parties' information about pivotality, or budgets relative to the value of office, could measure how much of this overpayment loss aversion amplifies.

From a public choice perspective, the results are also a statement about the incidence of political rents. The value of office in our game is fixed, and clientelist exchange determines only how much of it leaks to voters and through which channel. The model assigns that leakage to whoever proposes. Our participants instead spread it in a way that depends little on the institution: parties transfer part of the rent to voters whose votes do not matter, and voters who could claim most of it leave a large share with the winning party. Two implications follow. First, the distribution of clientelist rents may be less sensitive to institutional details about who initiates exchanges than bargaining models suggest, and more sensitive to how well participants condition on the decisiveness of individual votes. Second, the inefficiency of clientelism need not come only from targeting the wrong voters or from the deadweight cost of transfers; it can also come from buyers' imperfect assessment of when a vote is worth buying, which spends resources on votes that cannot change the outcome. Both implications are testable with designs that vary the information parties have about pivotality, holding the institution fixed.

The results also have a policy dimension. Anti-vote-buying laws and campaigns usually target buyers: they penalize parties and brokers who offer benefits, and they inform voters that the ballot is secret. If voters increasingly initiate exchanges, one might expect enforcement aimed at buyers to become less effective, and the incidence of the rents created by clientelism to shift toward voters. Our results suggest caution on both counts. When voters set prices, they ask for less than they could obtain, and parties still pay for votes that do not matter. The total volume of transfers is therefore not obviously smaller under voter initiative, and the voters who initiate do not appear to capture more of the surplus. To the extent that our laboratory behavior generalizes, the most effective margin for policy may be the parties' willingness to pay for non-pivotal votes---for instance, by making the electoral irrelevance of small blocs of votes more salient---rather than the question of who proposes the exchange.

Our design has limitations that also define the scope of these conclusions. The game is one-shot, with one voter and two parties, so it abstracts from repeated interaction, reputation, brokers, and competition among sellers. Votes are bound to transactions by design, which removes the commitment problem at the heart of real clientelism. The main comparison is adequately powered; estimates of behavior within the price-setting-voter institution, with \Sexpr{sVS} sessions and \Sexpr{pivVS} pivotal voter-rounds, are less precise.

These limitations suggest the next steps. Estimating quantal-response or level-$k$ versions of both games would quantify how much of the gap between theory and behavior is due to noise in parties' acceptance decisions and how much to voters' beliefs about it. Designs that let several voters compete as sellers, or several brokers compete as buyers, would show whether competition restores the power that our voters left unused, as competition does in other bargaining experiments \parencite{Roth1991}. And field designs that observe both requests and offers in the same clientelist networks could test whether the offsetting frictions we document also equalize the gains from vote selling and vote buying outside the laboratory.

